// A `Uint8Array` view over a gandr buffer, which Web Crypto fills in place.
//
// A copy of each byte across the boundary costs as much as the work itself.
// So a numeric sequence crosses by layout: JavaScript reads and writes
// gandr's memory through a view. The view is sound only when its layout
// matches the buffer's, and when no gandr alias reads the buffer during the
// call.
//
// The buffer is `Bytes n` in linear memory, and its row states the view's
// layout: element `U8`, length `n`, at the buffer's address. The boundary
// check admits the operation against that row, as it admits a niche row for
// a C caller. The buffer crosses at grade one and returns as a second
// output, so no gandr name holds it while the host writes. A `Float32Array`
// over `f32` elements has the same form with element `F32`.
//
// - see: #4883 — the web boundary sketch, its shape in the issue body
// - see: `../../model/interop/boundary/by-layout.gandr` — a row read by layout
// - see: `../abi-and-boundaries/computed-row.gandr` — a row the importer computes

// The row vocabulary of the boundary check.
import std.boundary (Arg, NoCode, Row, View);

// The scalar that codes an element.
import std.layout (U8);

// The bytes a buffer holds.
import std.buffer (Bytes);

/// The row of `getRandomValues`. It takes a view of `n` `U8` elements and
/// answers the same view.
pub def randomRow (n : Nat) -> Row = (Row (Arg (View U8 n) NoCode)) (View U8 n)

/// The Web Crypto operation that fills a view with random bytes.
@[boundary(randomRow)]
extern JavaScript from "webidl:webcrypto" as Crypto where
  /// The buffer, filled in place and handed back.
  def getRandomValues [n : Nat] (buffer[1] : Bytes n) -> (buffer[1] : Bytes n)

/// A fresh key of 32 random bytes.
pub def key () -> [Crypto] Bytes 32 =
  let buffer = Bytes.zeroed 32
  Crypto.getRandomValues buffer
